[Music]  last week police  in washington state made an arrest in  the 30-year-old killing of a canadian  couple  she had been sexually assaulted and shot  in the back of the head  he had been beaten and strangled  there was plenty of dna evidence but  nobody really  to point a finger off  [Music]  for 31 years there was basically no  resolution but then the power of using  genetics and genealogy was able to  get the name of a suspect within a day  because of the sort of analysis we can  now do  you can discover things about the  relatives of people who have  uploaded their genetic information to  the internet and i think that's  what's both the power of this method and  also the thing that raises a lot of  these  disquieting questions about privacy and  consent  that we're going through right now we're  evolving how dna shows who you're  connected to  [Music]  genetics is this thing that we think of  as cutting edge science you know it's  happening in some of the most  advanced labs in the world and genealogy  is something that you know maybe  makes you think of your grandparents you  know looking up records maybe going to  churches  and looking at gravestones to kind of  try and trace your family tree back  so genetic genealogy is what happens  when you combine  those two sciences a family tree  represents so much more than history  so in the mid 2000s  this new industry started to grow  based on the rise of an of a consumer  dna testing  uh industry um and that was something  that came out of the sequencing of a  human genome  basically companies trying to figure out  how to make this a consumer product  i decided to have my dna tested through  ancestry dna the big surprise was  we're not german at all  family tree dna was the first company on  the scene  they were really targeting hardcore  genealogists and then you have companies  that are more targeted  at a broader commercial audience start  coming into the scene  now jed match really was an important  breakthrough  in genetic genealogy because what it did  was it created an  open source site where if you had done  ancestry's  dna tests you could also compare your  results to  people who had done other tna tests but  you know my dna  isn't just my dna it's my cousin's dna  it's my mom's dna  and so when i make a decision to share  my dna  i'm also making a decision to share  their dna  so uh at this point  at least 30 million people have have  taken these consumer dna  tests some large portion of those people  have also  uploaded the results to these public  databases  that are searchable by anyone who's  curious  30 million might not seem like a big  number to you  of course you know there's billions of  people in the world  but the reason that number is  significant is that  you don't necessarily have to have  shared your genetic information  in order for your information to be  exposed so  there was one study that found that  just two percent of people in the world  would need to share their genetic  information for us to potentially be  able  to identify everybody  i'm joined right now with genetic  genealog genealogist that's a mouthful  right there cece moore  hi how are you it's great to meet you  i'm happy to be here so  cece moore is probably one of the most  interesting people that i have  ever interviewed she started off her  career as a model  actress at one point she  made a living playing barbie and at one  point  she decided that she wanted to  make a family tree as a gift for her  niece  who was getting married this sort of  became  this pretty all-consuming uh pastime for  her  and she didn't have you know formal  scientific training it's just that she  was doing a lot of this and through sort  of  trial and error she realized the way  that you could merge  these traditional genealogical methods  with the genetic information and also um  social media she came up with this idea  of triangulating  your relatives to discover the hidden  identity of someone  and then she became the genetic  genealogist on this tv show called  finding your roots so take a look at  this family tree  this is christian kumal and teresa  stockhausen we traced another of their  descendants  and found one of their great-grandsons  who was willing to test for us  so as cece moore is helping all these  adoptees find their birth parents  people begin asking the question of what  else  can we use this technique for and  there's one  really obvious answer to what else you  can use that for  and that is figuring out who committed a  crime  june 18th 1976 marked the first  known rape by the east area rapist  the golden state killer was this serial  murderer rapist who terrorized  california in the 70s  there was plenty of dna evidence but  nobody really  to point a finger off so it became a  cold case and then  uh in the spring of 2018  there was this announcement of the  solving of this  uh decades-old case that had used these  methods  to basically identify the suspect  cece moore sees this as a green light  she gets in contact with this company  called parabon which can take really  degraded dna samples and extract  information out of them and make the  same sort of genetic  profile the same kind of file that you  would get from a 23andme or an ancestry  she had already been in contact with a  few  police departments and detectives across  the country  including detectives in snohomish county  washington where there had been a  very brutal murder  [Music]  21 year old jay cook and 18 year old  tanya van kylenburg  were murdered while visiting seattle  from victoria british columbia  there were a few clues there was this  blue blanket that the  one of the bodies had been wrapped in  and there was dna  in 1987 happens to be the very dawn  of the dna analysis era in law  enforcement  but there are really severe limitations  to that it's really more of a  confirmation tool  than like a true detective tool and even  as these databases grew  there weren't any matches this should  tell us which ones we can be more  confident  about cece gets to work doing her part  which is trying to figure out how  what kinds of records and stuff you can  use to build out this family tree  not every crime scene dna sample will  have a relative that has uploaded their  genetic information  so you kind of have to win the lottery  and cece  won big she didn't just get one match  she got two so basically whoever  gave that sample the suspect has  two relatives in the ged match database  and they're pretty good  matches in the context of genetic  genealogy they're they're second cousins  and when she built out the family trees  there was only  one male that was the right amount of  relatedness  to the genetic hits and dead match and  they knew from other evidence that they  were looking for a mail so it was  very strong indication very quickly of  who their suspect might be  the suspect happened to be a truck  driver so they followed  him around in his truck for a few days  and that at one point  a coffee cup falls out of the truck when  he's pulling away from a traffic light  and they are able to test the saliva on  the lip  of the coffee cup and it matches  yesterday we took into custody a  55-year-old seatac man  who is suspected of the 1987 murders of  jay cooke  and tanya van kylenburg we the jury find  the defendant william earl talbott ii  guilty of defense  it's an incredible testament to the  power  of these tools given the right kind of  data someone  with cc's abilities is able to sift  through an enormously large data set  and come back with a single name  so to go from entertainment and then  end up in a science career in my 40s is  definitely not something i would have  ever imagined without genetic genealogy  this case never would have been solved  cc moore did in two hours  what 20 or 30 cops couldn't do in 30  years  i think most people don't realize the  power  of these types of tests when this  happened  there was sort of this agreement okay we  can use this very  powerful technology to solve cold cases  but very quickly people started to use  the technology  for other things for uh murders that  were not long unsolved and i think when  that happened  that started to raise the level of  discomfort people had  the particularly sticky thing here is  that you're not just sharing your  information you're sharing your family's  information  and that's a tough thing especially when  we know things like  the criminal justice system  disproportionately  targets minorities right or when we know  that sometimes dna  evidence is wrong  and i think another part of the  cautionary aspect of this is that this  is a  this is an application of genetic  analysis that nobody dreamed of  when we set out to  sequence the human genome and it's only  through this combination of these  obsessive people with their weird  interests discovering this tool  that we have ended up here and so i  think  there's bound to be other totally  unforeseen uses for this  that we can't even imagine right now  somebody  might find out you know about your  family  health history for example a life  insurer if they knew that you were prone  to heart attacks  they would be able to use that  information to deny you a policy  so it's not just you know crime suspects  that face exposure with this information  all of us could be exposed in one way or  another once this information is out  there  it's an enormously rich data trove that  a lot of people have decided to make  public  and perhaps the people who've uploaded  it haven't fully thought through  the implications  you

